  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington is running below pace on a capacity shortfall. Yesterday's 13% revenue miss ($3,019 vs $3,463) traces directly to five groomers producing $433/groomer\u201416% below your trailing-30-day baseline of $500/groomer/day. The team executed well; the math didn't work. Add-on attachment cratered from 58% to 35%, signaling either booking pressure (no time for upsells) or front-of-house discipline slip\u2014worth a brief floor check. Retail dropped 25%, consistent with transaction count down 18%, so that's volume-driven, not conversion. Month-to-date you're flat on a nominal basis but actually 3.2% behind calendar-adjusted expectation, which is real softness. The bright spot: your 12-week cadence bucket is shrinking (15.5% to 13.3%), suggesting retention is recovering and churn risk is declining.\n\nHicksville is outperforming and trending the right direction. Yesterday's 39% revenue surge ($1,304 vs $935) with 41% more transactions signals genuine demand lift, and you're running 5.3% *ahead* of DOW-adjusted expectation for the month. Three groomers hit $357/groomer\u2014still 13% below trailing baseline\u2014but that's the constraint, not the problem; the problem is you don't have capacity to capture it. Add-on attach is healthy at 58%. This is a store ready for staffing investment. The concern: your 12-week cadence bucket is *growing* (10.2% to 12.6%), which is early stealth churn. Watch customer messaging and rebooking discipline there.\n\nThe pattern across both stores: you're capacity-constrained at Port Washington (solid baseline, insufficient heads) and capacity-constrained at Hicksville (strong demand, nowhere to go). Neither is a demand problem. Today you have 10 groomer-days scheduled across both locations; tomorrow drops to 6\u2014uneven. Next seven days: 48 groomer-days combined, or 6.9 per day, which matches recent run rate but leaves both stores light relative to indicated demand."
